I agree.  I think that being "punished" for misspelling something is
not quite in line with the concept of the game.  I mean, after all, if
I release a genie from a bottle, does he ask me to spell my wish?  No,
I just say "Hey, dude, I'd love a really good piece of armor,
something that would, like, increase my strength a whole lot.  Got any
of that?"  

Yes, I know all the classic stories about being careful for what you
wish for (where the third wish is used to neutralize the first two
wishes, leaving the wisher in exactly the same condition he was in
before he got the wishes), but I don't this this is very nethack-like.

I like the idea of having a list of things to wish for, but
I do think that if you are going to provide a list of possible 
things to wish for, such as:

a) a long sword
b) a long sword named Fire Brand
c) a long sword named Excalibur

...etc...

then there should be some choices which are "bad" or "neutral" to
make up for the added information.  For example, add to the list
some long swords with special names, but negative benefits,
like:

g) a long sword named Joe
h) a long sword named Dan Quayle   
i) a long sword named Sleeper      


where wielding h) drops your intelligence, and wielding i) is like
wearing a amulet of restful sleep.

Comments?

